    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. To make it more clear for the students, could you go into more detail about the moment the cell walls rupture? I'm particularly interested in describing that sudden, almost \"aggressive\" release of the gas when the knife hits the onion. How would you describe the speed and nature of that chemical transition?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the knife cuts through an onion cell, it disrupts the cell membrane, causing the cell walls to rupture. This rupture leads to a rapid diffusion of the encapsulated enzymes, including alliinase, into the surrounding solution, where they can come into contact with the alliin molecules and begin the reaction. As the alliinase is released, the alliin molecules rapidly hydrolyze to form syn-propanethial-S-oxide, a sulfur-containing gas with a pungent odor and extremely unpleasant stinging properties. This process unfolds extremely quickly, up to a minute, meaning that in a matter of milliseconds, the enzymes and reactants are released into the surrounding air, producing the characteristic stinging sensation caused by the rapid release of syn-propanethial-S-oxide gas.",
      "source": "target",
